% composition-series: Ontario Listening Bridges — set 1
% composition-grade: JK
% composition-elements: beat, voice and volume (Kindergarten Program 2016) — an immediate exact echo of a two-bar sung greeting, with one binary choice each half: a loud or soft echo, then a sung or clapped echo
% composition-listen: Ella Jenkins — "You'll Sing a Song and I'll Sing a Song", title track of the album You'll Sing a Song and I'll Sing a Song, with members of the Urban Gateways Children's Chorus (Folkways FC 7664, 1966; Smithsonian Folkways SFW 45010 reissue)
% composition-listen-url: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u60cDWfAMBo
% composition-bridge: Notice how a leader leaves room for a real answer; compare an exact echo with an answer that changes one thing, such as how loud it is.
% composition-context: Ella Jenkins (1924-2024), a Chicago-based singer and educator, made call-and-response with children central to her recordings; this 1966 album was added to the Library of Congress National Recording Registry in 2007. This tiny original greeting is a listening doorway, not a transcription of her melody or words.
% composition-distance: Original melody, words and form; the named recording is for listening and discussion only and is linked from the Smithsonian Folkways auto-generated channel, not embedded. Do not label this as an Ella Jenkins song.
% improv: {"id":"A","bars":"1-4","role":"teacher sings, class echoes","prompt":"Our echo will be…","choices":["loud","soft"],"replyBars":2}
% improv: {"id":"B","bars":"5-8","role":"teacher or child leader, class echoes","prompt":"Our echo will be…","choices":["sung","clapped"],"replyBars":2}
% dossier-function: original JK greeting and echo song; a leader sings a two-bar greeting and the class echoes it at once, choosing loud or soft for the first echo and singing or clapping the rhythm for the second
% dossier-elements: eight bars in 2/4 (L:1/8), F major, pitch set F-G-A (do-re-mi), written range F4-A4 (a major third). Bars 1-2 call "Hel-lo, how are you?" (A4 F4 quarters, G4 G4 eighths, F4 quarter); bars 3-4 the class echoes it exactly; bars 5-6 a second call "Glad to see you here to-day" (A A G G eighths, G G eighths, F quarter) that varies the opening and keeps the same ending; bars 7-8 the class echoes it. Form: call-echo, call-echo (a a b b, with b ending like a). Note values: quarters and eighths only, no rests; every phrase ends on the tonic F4. Largest interval a major third (A4-F4). Chords F and C (I and V) for the teacher or Band only
% dossier-practice: Start at quarter = 76 and reach the written 88 once the echo lands on the downbeat. Rehearsal key F major; the written range F4-A4 is inside the JK comfort zone (about D4-A4) and the young-unison C4-D5 range. Teach in steps: the teacher sings the call and gestures "your turn" with an open hand on the last note; children echo; then show a loud or soft hand sign before bar 1 (big arms, small hands); then clap the rhythm of the words instead of singing. A child may become the leader once the class echoes reliably. Breathe on each final quarter note
% dossier-accompaniment: Newly authored, not traditional: F and C chords (I and V), one or two per bar. The folk style gives a light strummed accompaniment; it does not imitate Ella Jenkins or her recording, and the Band may be muted so the voices are heard
% dossier-lyrics: Original English words written for this record: "Hello, how are you? / Glad to see you here today." One syllable per note, stresses on the beats ("HEL-lo", "how are YOU", "GLAD to SEE you", "here to-DAY"), sung by leader and class. Teachers may swap in a child's name for "you" or greet in a home language of the class; record those as classroom adaptations
% dossier-honesty: Original AI-assisted course composition generated for Grand Central Stationery on 11 November 2026 under human-directed range, rhythm and teaching constraints. It is not traditional, not collected from a community and not modelled on Ella Jenkins's melody or words; her recording is a listening bridge only
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0. Grand Central Stationery dedicates the melody, words, chords and this ABC encoding to the public domain to the extent legally possible. Ella Jenkins's song and recording remain the copyright of their owners (Smithsonian Folkways Recordings and her publisher) and are not included
% dossier-respect: Credit Ella Jenkins by name in the lesson, say she was a Black American educator from Chicago who spent decades singing with children, and listen to her own recording rather than presenting this greeting as a substitute or as her work
% dossier-review: Early-years (JK) music teacher, JK classroom trial and a second score reader; all reviews still required
