N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition; this is the compositional audit. Every bar totals four eighths (2/4). Bars 1 and 3: A4 F4 quarters; bars 2, 4, 6 and 8: G4 G4 eighths, F4 quarter; bars 5 and 7: A4 A4 G4 G4 eighths. Pitch set F-G-A, range F4-A4, largest interval a major third (A4 down to F4). The earlier draft left bar 4 empty, which cut the echo in half and put "Your turn" on notes the class should be singing; now bars 3-4 and 7-8 are the class's exact echoes, and the words fit one syllable per note. The analyzer reads level 1; its Ontario line reads Grade 5 only because it counts the I-V chord symbols, which are for the teacher or Band, not the children. Originality: mi-do and re-re-do greetings are common property; checked against "Bounce High, Bounce Low", "Hot Cross Buns", "Good Morning to You" ("Happy Birthday") and "Frère Jacques", with no shared opening. Ella Jenkins's "You'll Sing a Song and I'll Sing a Song" has a different melody, words and form; nothing is taken.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether JK children can echo on the very next downbeat with no rest at quarter = 88, whether they hear the loud/soft choice as an echo that changes one thing, and whether four-year-olds can clap the "how are you" eighths. A classroom trial, an early-years music teacher and a second score reader must settle these.
